Published ports now reach a guest loopback service (2026-10-26, 2026-10-27)
============================================================================

Found from the first real use of the new flag: `mbox-online --expose-port 4321`
followed by an `npm run dev` that printed `http://localhost:4321/en/` and was not
reachable from the host. The alias expands to

  mbox --host-loopback -p 8000 -p 8012 -p 8080 -p 8545 --network open --expose-port 4321

So the flag arrives after `--network open`, which is fine. Host-side assembly for
exactly that shape:

  $ mbox --dry-run /tmp --host-loopback -p 8000 ... --network open --expose-port 14321
  ... --net public --net-rule allow:egress@host:tcp:8000 ... \
      --port 127.0.0.1:14321:14321 -- /nix/store/...-mbox-host-loopback \ 8000\ 8012\ 8080\ 8545 ...

The guest relay is handed only the `-p` ports, so publishing does not interact with
--host-loopback at all.

Diagnosis, real VM, same invocation, only the guest bind address differs:

  A) guest: busybox httpd -f -p 14321 -h /tmp/www          # binds 0.0.0.0
     $ curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:14321/
     A-PAGE

  B) guest: busybox httpd -f -p 127.0.0.1:14322 -h /tmp/wwwb   # loopback only
     guest netstat -tln:
       tcp        0      0 127.0.0.1:14322         0.0.0.0:*               LISTEN
     guest self-fetch:
       $ busybox wget -q -O - http://127.0.0.1:14322/
       B-PAGE
     host fetch of the published port:
       $ curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:14322/
       curl: (52) Empty reply from server

msb's port publisher dials the guest's IPv4 address, not the guest's loopback
(crates/network/lib/engine/ports/publisher.rs, the `guest_ip` field: "the common case
-- most services bind 0.0.0.0 or dual-stack ::, both of which accept v4"). A
loopback-only listener is therefore refused by the guest's own stack, and the host
sees a reset instead of a timeout. The forwarding on the host side is correct; the
missing hop is inside the guest.

That difference is still the diagnostic worth remembering:

  connection refused / timeout on the host   -> no host-side listener at all
                                                (flag missing, or VM gone)
  empty reply / ERR_EMPTY_RESPONSE           -> listener exists, guest bound 127.0.0.1

Fix: nothing asked of the caller and nothing asked of the service. The wrapper
starts programs/mbox/publish-forward.sh.in before the user's command whenever a
port is published. It reads the guest's own `/proc/net/tcp{,6}` every 0.2 s and,
for a published port whose *only* listener is on the guest's loopback, binds the
guest's own address on that port and forwards into the loop; when the loopback
listener goes away it closes that socket again. A port a service already serves on
the guest's address, on 0.0.0.0 or on a dual-stack :: is left completely alone, so
the common case never has a stranger on its port. No nftables/iptables and no
kernel feature is assumed; the addresses are the guest's, so the host's published
loopback port is unchanged.

The decision is made from listening sockets, not from configuration, so it holds for
any port, any service, and any start order, including a service that restarts with a
different bind address.

Two bugs were found and fixed while verifying:

  1. The relay counted its *own* listener on the guest address as proof that the
     service was reachable, so it withdrew and rebuilt the relay on every poll
     (0.2 s) and host requests failed most of the time. Excluding the relay's own
     address once it is bound removed the oscillation.
  2. That flap was invisible in the first VM run because the guest was still running
     an older realised copy of the script: the wrapper embeds a store path, so a
     stale `/tmp` wrapper keeps testing the previous version. Re-evaluating the
     wrapper and realising the new derivation is part of the check.

Verified in a real VM, the user's own alias shape
(`--host-loopback -p 8000 --network open --expose-port PORT`), server bound to the
guest's 127.0.0.1, guest listeners unchanged:

  $ curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:14334/      # 8 rounds, 1 s apart
  ALIAS-PAGE  ALIAS-PAGE  ALIAS-PAGE  ALIAS-PAGE
  ALIAS-PAGE  ALIAS-PAGE  ALIAS-PAGE  ALIAS-PAGE
  successful rounds: 8/8
  guest listeners:
    tcp  0  0 127.0.0.1:14334  0.0.0.0:*  LISTEN

and, with `--network blocked` (no internet at all):

  guest `python3 -m http.server 14331 --bind 127.0.0.1`   host -> LOOPBACK-PAGE
  guest `python3 -m http.server 14332 --bind 0.0.0.0`     host -> NET-PAGE
        (started 3 s after the forwarder was already watching: the service got the
        port, nothing had taken it, and its access log shows the host connection
        arriving directly)
  guest `python3 -m http.server 14333 --bind ::1`         host -> V6-PAGE

tests/mbox-publish-forward.py covers the same state machine without a VM: forward a
loopback-only listener, one announcement only under repeated polls, leave a
wildcard listener alone even when it appears after the first poll, forward ::1,
withdraw when the listener stops, and let the freed port accept a listener on the
guest's address.
